TASK 1 – ALGORITHM DESIGN
Title: Design, Analyse and Validate an Efficient Search Strategy for a Large-Scale Student Record System
1. Aim
To design three different searching algorithms for locating a student's registration number in a university database and compare their efficiency for different dataset sizes.

2. Algorithm 1: Linear Search

Design Strategy: Iterative Searching

Basic Idea:
Linear search checks each student's registration number one by one until the target registration number is found or all records have been checked.

Pseudocode:

LINEAR_SEARCH(records, target)
    FOR i = 0 TO length(records) - 1
        IF records[i].registration_number = target
            RETURN records[i]
    RETURN NOT_FOUND

Preprocessing Required:
No sorting or preprocessing is required.

Justification:
Linear search works with both sorted and unsorted records. It is easy to implement and provides a baseline for comparing the performance of other searching algorithms.

3. Algorithm 2: Recursive Binary Search

Design Strategy: Divide and Conquer

Basic Idea:
Binary search compares the target registration number with the middle element of a sorted list. If the target is not found, it recursively searches only the half that may contain the target.

Pseudocode:

BINARY_SEARCH(records, low, high, target)
    IF low > high
        RETURN NOT_FOUND

    mid = floor((low + high) / 2)

    IF records[mid].registration_number = target
        RETURN records[mid]

    ELSE IF target < records[mid].registration_number
        RETURN BINARY_SEARCH(records, low, mid - 1, target)

    ELSE
        RETURN BINARY_SEARCH(records, mid + 1, high, target)

Preprocessing Required:
The records must be sorted by registration number before searching. If the records are initially unsorted, sorting generally requires O(n log n) time.

Justification:
Binary search reduces the search range by half during each recursive call. It is efficient for searching large, sorted datasets.

4. Algorithm 3: Hash Table Search

Design Strategy: Hash-Based Searching

Basic Idea:
A hash table stores student registration numbers as keys and their corresponding student records as values. A search uses the target registration number to retrieve the matching record.

Pseudocode:

BUILD_HASH_TABLE(records)
    CREATE an empty hash table

    FOR each record IN records
        hash_table[record.registration_number] = record

    RETURN hash_table

HASH_SEARCH(hash_table, target)
    IF target exists in hash_table
        RETURN hash_table[target]

    RETURN NOT_FOUND

Preprocessing Required:
A hash table must be constructed from the student records. Its expected construction time is O(n), and it requires O(n) additional space.

Justification:
Hash table search provides expected constant-time lookup under suitable hashing assumptions. It is useful when the system handles many search requests.

5. Initial Comparison

Linear search is simple and requires no preprocessing, but it can examine every record. Recursive binary search is efficient for sorted records but requires sorting if the data is initially unsorted. Hash table search requires additional memory and construction time but offers expected constant-time lookup.

The final algorithm will be recommended after mathematical analysis, implementation, and experimental testing.
